h('Abstract',1)
para('This research develops a Nigeria-focused design and evidence synthesis for an adaptive cyber-physical framework to detect and mitigate stealthy man-in-the-middle (MiTM) attacks in power-system communications. It adopts Wlazlo et al. (2021) as the principal baseline. That study used an emulation-based cyber-physical testbed and a synthetic 2,000-bus grid to examine Distributed Network Protocol 3 (DNP3)-related false-data injection (FDI), false-command injection (FCI), and replay, combining Snort intrusion-detection system (IDS) alerts with network monitoring. The proposed extension follows the supplied research proposal: compare rule-based, network-only data-driven, and adaptive cross-layer detectors; test normal disturbances and held-out conditions; and assess mitigation separately under explicit safety constraints. The methodology specified is a quantitative controlled experiment in an isolated, authorised simulation or testbed, with time-aligned network and physical telemetry. The present deliverable completes the baseline analysis, Nigerian context synthesis, framework specification, variable operationalisation, scenario matrix, and evaluation plan. It does not report primary experimental performance because no testbed output or labelled dataset was supplied or generated. The results chapter therefore reports verifiable findings from the baseline and Nigerian policy/industry evidence, presents the framework design outcome, and marks all four hypotheses as untested. Nigeria’s supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA) integration programme and the operational conditions documented by the Nigerian Electricity Regulatory Commission support prioritising local benign variation, including communication delay, loss, and grid-state changes, in future validation. The main contribution is a reproducible and safety-oriented research protocol that can be implemented without claiming unmeasured gains.\n\nKeywords: man-in-the-middle attack, power system, operational technology, DNP3, adaptive detection, Nigeria, SCADA')

