h('1 Introduction',1)
para('Electric power systems depend on operational technology (OT), supervisory control and data acquisition (SCADA), intelligent electronic devices, field sensors, communication links, and control-room software. They use measured data to estimate system state and issue commands to field equipment. This coupling makes the power grid a cyber-physical system: a change to digital messages can change what operators believe about the grid or what a controller asks equipment to do. OT security must therefore protect integrity and availability while respecting process timing, reliability, and physical safety (Stouffer et al., 2023).')
para('A MiTM adversary positions itself in a communication path and can observe, delay, replay, or modify messages while making the exchange appear legitimate. In the power-system setting, this position can support false-data injection (FDI), false-command injection (FCI), and replay. A detector that relies on network timing alone may confuse malicious delay with ordinary communications variation. A detector that relies only on the physical process may also confuse malicious manipulation with a real disturbance. The research question is whether a controlled, adaptive combination of the two evidence sources can improve detection and support a safe response.')
para('This study follows the supplied research proposal and explicitly uses Wlazlo et al. (2021) as its baseline. The contribution is a research design and framework extension, not a claim that cross-layer machine learning has already outperformed the baseline. The design separates detection from mitigation, compares specified methods on a common scenario matrix, and treats false alarms and operational disruption as outcomes alongside classification performance.')

h('1.1 Background of the Study',2)
para('Wlazlo et al. (2021) demonstrated multi-stage MiTM attacks in an emulation-based power-system testbed. Their scenarios included FDI, FCI, and replay over DNP3, and their work combined intrusion-detection alerts with network measures such as round-trip time (RTT), retransmission behaviour, and delay. The article explains why timing signatures are difficult to interpret: industrial control system (ICS) applications have different communication rates, and added MiTM latency can be masked by legitimate network behaviour. It also demonstrates that digital manipulation can have physical consequences, so network alerts should be interpreted in the operating context of the controlled process.')
para('Nigeria is a relevant setting because the electricity system is in a period of control-system integration. The Nigerian Electricity Regulatory Commission (NERC) reported in December 2024 that transmission and distribution systems were being integrated into the Transmission Company of Nigeria (TCN) SCADA and electricity management system (EMS), with generation integration under discussion (NERC, 2024). Increased supervisory visibility and remote coordination make cyber-physical monitoring and disciplined incident response consequential design requirements. The statement is about the architecture and transition, not evidence that Nigerian utilities have experienced the specific attacks studied here.')
para('NERC’s 2024 annual report recorded nine grid collapse incidents in 2024 and documented frequency behaviour outside the normal 49.75–50.25 Hz band during the year; the report linked this operational context in part to the lack of a SCADA system providing real-time monitoring and load management (NERC, 2025). These reported values make an important methodological point: local detectors must not label every voltage or frequency deviation as an intrusion. The experiment must include benign operating changes and distinguish the measured response from the cause of a deviation. A Nigerian cybersecurity assessment similarly describes the sector’s mix of legacy infrastructure and newer digital metering and automated control, and recommends coordinated people, process, and technology capabilities (Ogundari et al., 2021).')
para('Nigeria’s National Cybersecurity Policy and Strategy calls for critical information infrastructure protection through risk management, stakeholder coordination, mitigation planning, incident response, and resilience (Nigeria Computer Emergency Response Team [ngCERT], 2021). This supports a design that includes monitoring, operator decision support, containment, recovery, and post-incident learning. The framework in this research therefore focuses on authorised laboratory evaluation and is designed to inform, rather than replace, utility engineering judgement and national policy.')

h('1.2 Statement of the Research Problem',2)
para('Stealthy MiTM activity can preserve enough normal protocol behaviour to evade simple alarms while altering, delaying, or replaying control messages. Network indicators such as latency and retransmission rate are useful but may be ambiguous under ordinary traffic load, packet loss, clock variation, or routing changes. Physical measurements provide additional context, but physical disturbances can also be benign and should not by themselves trigger an attack label. A detector that ignores either evidence source risks missed attacks or excessive false alarms.')
para('The baseline paper provides testbed-based attack scenarios and network-oriented detection mechanisms, but it does not answer the proposal’s narrower comparative question: whether adaptive correlation of network telemetry with physical measurements generalises across changed operating conditions and whether a safety-aware response reduces physical impact without causing unacceptable disruption. Detection performance and mitigation safety need separate evaluation. The research problem is therefore to specify and validate a fair, reproducible comparison among rule-based, network-only, and adaptive cross-layer methods against stealthy MiTM scenarios in normal and changed conditions, using measures that capture both cyber detection and physical consequences.')

h('1.3 Aim, Objectives, Research Questions and Hypotheses',2)
h('1.3.1 Aim',3)
para('To design and evaluate an adaptive cyber-physical detection and mitigation framework for stealthy MiTM attacks in power-system communication and control environments, using Wlazlo et al. (2021) as the baseline and adapting the experimental context to Nigeria.')
h('1.3.2 Specific Objectives',3)
for t in ['1. Critically analyse the baseline study’s attack scenarios, testbed approach, detection indicators, findings, and limitations.','2. Identify candidate network and physical features that may separate malicious manipulation from ordinary network and grid variation.','3. Specify an adaptive detector that combines aligned cyber and physical evidence and produces an interpretable alert or confidence score.','4. Compare that detector with rule-based and network-only data-driven baselines under the same scenario matrix.','5. Evaluate precision, recall, F1-score (the harmonic mean of precision and recall), false-positive rate, detection latency, computational overhead, generalisation, and mitigation impact.']:
    para(t)
h('1.3.3 Research Questions',3)
for t in ['1. What methods and indicators does Wlazlo et al. (2021) establish, and what unresolved questions motivate the proposed extension?','2. Does combining network telemetry with physical-system measurements improve MiTM detection over a network-only detector under the selected test conditions?','3. How does the proposed framework perform on attack configurations or operating conditions withheld from training?','4. Can cross-layer evidence reduce false alarms caused by legitimate delays, packet loss, or physical disturbances without reducing attack recall?','5. Does a predefined safety-aware mitigation policy reduce measured attack impact without unacceptable disruption to legitimate control?']:
    para(t)
h('1.3.4 Research Hypotheses',3)
for t in ['H1. Under the selected testbed conditions, an adaptive detector using network and physical features will achieve a higher F1-score than the network-only baseline.','H2. At comparable attack recall, the proposed detector will have a lower false-positive rate than the rule-based baseline.','H3. Performance will decline on changed conditions, but the decline will be smaller for the adaptive approach than for a non-adaptive baseline.','H4. A safety-aware mitigation policy will reduce measured physical impact relative to detection without mitigation while remaining within predefined operating constraints.']:
    para(t)
para('These hypotheses are directional predictions from the proposal. They are not reported as supported because no primary experiment was run for this research work.')

h('1.4 Significance of the Study',2)
para('The study provides an explicit, testable extension of Wlazlo et al. (2021): it retains the baseline’s MiTM scenario families and network evidence, then adds physical-state correlation, held-out operating conditions, and separate mitigation evaluation. For researchers, it defines a common comparison plan and guards against data leakage and accuracy-only reporting. For Nigerian power-sector stakeholders, it translates the research into a controlled way to assess monitoring during SCADA integration, where benign operating variation and reliability constraints must be represented in the test data. For policy and engineering practice, it aligns an alert-to-recovery workflow with the risk management and resilience orientation of ngCERT’s national strategy (ngCERT, 2021).')
para('The study’s practical value will depend on validation with a suitable authorised testbed, transparent data, and utility review of operational limits. It is not a claim that a detector can prevent grid disturbances, nor that an ML alert should automatically operate a breaker.')

h('1.5 Scope and Limitations',2)
para('The scope is laboratory or simulation-based evaluation of MiTM-related replay, FDI, and FCI scenarios in power-system communications. The framework considers network telemetry, selected physical measurements, intrusion-detection alerts, and constrained mitigation. DNP3 is retained as the baseline protocol because it is central to Wlazlo et al. (2021). Any extension to Modbus or IEC 61850 should be treated as a separately documented protocol-specific validation rather than assumed to transfer automatically.')
para('The work is Nigeria-oriented in its sector context, normal-condition design, operational measures, and policy implications. It does not use utility operational data, identify a real Nigerian utility vulnerability, or conduct any activity on a live grid. Its present evidence base is documentary and analytical: the supplied proposal and baseline paper, peer-reviewed and standards literature, and Nigerian regulatory and policy publications. Because no experimental output was supplied or created, measured classification scores, latency, mitigation effectiveness, statistical tests, and hypothesis decisions remain outside the empirical claims of this document.')
para('A simulated or emulated model cannot represent every protection scheme, topology, vendor implementation, communications failure, or operator practice in Nigerian utilities. Results from a future testbed would need careful limits, repeated trials, scenario diversity, and utility-specific safety review before operational interpretation.')

h('2 Literature Review',1)
h('2.1 MiTM Attacks in Power-System Control',2)
para('MiTM describes an adversary’s position in a communication exchange; FDI and FCI describe manipulations made possible from that position. Replay reuses previously valid information or commands. These categories overlap in an attack chain but should remain distinct in scenario labels: the attack’s access path, manipulated message, protocol exchange, and physical consequence are different variables. Wlazlo et al. (2021) provide the principal exemplar, using DNP3 communications and an emulated large synthetic grid to connect multi-stage MiTM activity with misleading measurements and control actions.')
para('The baseline paper also distinguishes the network and process sides of detection. It uses Snort alerts and network monitoring, while collecting data from the emulated communication environment, power-system simulator, and DNP3 masters. Its motivation is practical: conventional tools may not support industrial protocols well, and added latency alone may be indistinguishable from expected application timing. The baseline therefore supports multi-source monitoring and controlled testbed evaluation, but does not make a universal claim that one latency threshold detects every MiTM attack.')

h('2.2 False-Data Injection and Physical Evidence',2)
para('False measurement manipulation is a known challenge for power-system state estimation. Liu et al. (2011) formalised FDI attacks that can evade conventional bad-data detection under particular measurement and system assumptions. This work motivates evaluating state-related residuals and physical consistency, while also cautioning against treating a single residual as a guaranteed alarm. MiTM and FDI are related but not synonymous: a false measurement can arise through different access paths, and a MiTM may relay or replay messages without changing every value.')
para('Cross-layer evidence can help identify inconsistencies. Examples include a command that conflicts with an authenticated control sequence, a repeated measurement with an implausible timestamp, a network delay that coincides with a process residual shift, or a control action not reflected in the expected physical state. These are candidate features and relationships to test, not validated rules for all grid configurations. Their thresholds depend on sampling, control logic, engineering tolerances, and time synchronisation.')

h('2.3 Data-Driven Detection, Drift and Evaluation',2)
para('Machine-learning approaches can represent interactions among features that are cumbersome to encode as a single threshold. Cui et al. (2020) survey machine-learning methods for FDI detection in smart grids and identify evaluation and deployment challenges. The proposal correctly treats algorithm choice as empirical: logistic regression, random forests, boosted trees, and autoencoders are candidates, not established winners. For a modest testbed dataset, a calibrated tree ensemble is a reasonable pilot candidate because it supports nonlinear feature interactions and can be inspected with feature attribution; it must still be compared on identical data splits and calibrated for alert thresholds.')
para('A valid test split must prevent near-duplicate windows from the same scenario appearing in both training and testing. Grouping by attack configuration, operating state, or topology better tests generalisation than random row-level splitting. Because attacks may be rare, precision, recall, F1-score, false-positive rate, latency, and per-class results are more informative than accuracy alone. Repeated runs and confidence intervals should be used where feasible. A model’s behaviour when the distribution changes is part of the research question, not a side effect to omit.')

h('2.4 Safety-Aware OT Response',2)
para('OT security measures must account for timing, reliability, and safety alongside confidentiality and integrity (Stouffer et al., 2023). Detection and mitigation therefore require distinct evaluation. An alert can be evaluated using labels and detection latency; a response must additionally be judged by whether it reduces the attack-induced deviation, preserves legitimate control, avoids unsafe switching, and allows a validated recovery. Candidate actions in an isolated testbed include operator notification, holding or rejecting a command pending validation, simulated path isolation, or switching to a pre-approved fallback. The permissible response set must be defined by testbed owners before the experiment.')
para('The response lifecycle should include preparation, detection and analysis, containment, recovery, and post-incident review. This is consistent with the incident response orientation set out in Nigeria’s National Cybersecurity Policy and Strategy (ngCERT, 2021) and the people-process-technology emphasis reported by Ogundari et al. (2021). In this framework, adaptation is restricted to approved detector updates. The model may recalibrate within validated limits or flag drift for review; it may not silently learn from uncertain incident labels or autonomously change protection settings.')

h('2.5 Nigerian Electricity and Cybersecurity Context',2)
para('The Nigerian sector context makes benign variation a central design issue. NERC’s annual reporting characterises grid frequency, voltage fluctuation, transmission losses, and collapse incidents as system performance indicators. For 2024, the report recorded nine collapse incidents and monthly average upper and lower daily frequency bounds outside the normal operating band for the year (NERC, 2025). This establishes an operational reason to include ordinary disturbances and changing grid states in validation. It does not establish that any of those events were cyber caused.')
para('NERC’s December 2024 account of SCADA integration provides a concrete technology transition context: transmission and distribution systems were being integrated into a TCN SCADA/EMS platform, with generation integration being discussed (NERC, 2024). As this evolves, monitoring must work across real deployment boundaries, changing asset visibility, different vendors, and communications paths. Initial research should therefore model a clearly bounded segment and identify assumptions rather than claim full-grid generality.')
para('Ogundari et al. (2021) examined Nigerian electric power infrastructure using policy and technical sources and argued that legacy assets coexist with digital monitoring and control technologies. Their study is a national cybersecurity assessment, not an empirical report of MiTM incidents. It supports a risk-based, sector-specific research rationale. The national strategy also frames critical infrastructure resilience as a multi-stakeholder responsibility, joining risk management, mitigation, incident response, and recovery (ngCERT, 2021).')

h('2.6 Research Gap and Conceptual Position',2)
para('The gap is intentionally narrow. The baseline already demonstrates MiTM scenarios in a power-system cyber-physical testbed and uses multiple detection signals. The proposed research does not claim that MiTM detection, cyber-physical evidence, or response planning have never been studied. It asks for a controlled comparison of three specified detector classes, with changed operating conditions and benign disturbances, and a separate evaluation of safety-constrained mitigation. The additional Nigerian contribution is to make local operating variability, SCADA integration, and policy context explicit in the scenario and interpretation plan.')
para('The independent variables are detector type, attack type, traffic load, operating condition, and whether a scenario is represented in training. Dependent variables are classification performance, detection latency, computational overhead, and mitigation and physical-impact measures. Grid model, protocol implementation, sampling, duration, and normal-scenario definitions are controlled where possible. Packet loss, clock error, noise, random seed, topology change, and train-test distribution shift are potential confounders.')

