h('3.4 Data and Feature Collection',2)
para('The data record should synchronise packet metadata, protocol events, IDS alerts, control commands, measurements, and simulator state. Candidate network features based on the baseline include round-trip time, retransmission rate, packet inter-arrival time, protocol function or event anomalies, connection state, and IDS alert type. Candidate process features include voltage, frequency, power flow, breaker state, state-estimation residuals, and the expected response to a control action. The feature set must be selected before final testing and tied to each research question. Values should be sampled with timestamps, source labels, units, and missing-data indicators.')
para('A candidate source for a preliminary paired-data pilot is the SmartGrid-CyberPhysical-Attack-Dataset on Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers (IEEE) DataPort. Its record describes synchronized cyber and physical files for benign operation and several attack scenarios, including false-data injection. It can support building paired network-only and cross-layer model predictions on common test cases, subject to access terms and validation of its schema, labels, sampling, protocol, and scenario documentation (SmartGrid-CyberPhysical-Attack-Dataset, 2025). The listed scenarios do not establish coverage of the baseline’s replay and false-command injection cases or exact equivalence to Wlazlo et al.’s testbed. It is therefore a pilot resource, not a substitute for the proposed authorised, protocol-matched experiment.')
para('A ready-made paired detector-output file is not implied by the dataset record. The researcher should train both detectors using the same training and validation split, then save one row per shared test window with scenario_id, run_id, timestamp, true_label, network_only_prediction, and adaptive_cross_layer_prediction. Compute each model’s F1-score from these paired predictions at thresholds fixed before test evaluation. Keep splits grouped by scenario or run to prevent leakage.')
para('Time alignment is a prerequisite for cross-layer interpretation. The experiment should measure clock offset and report how windows are formed around each event. If clock quality falls outside a predefined tolerance, that run should be flagged or excluded according to a rule set before outcome review. Data should be partitioned by run or scenario so that overlapping windows and near-identical attack traces do not leak across training and test partitions.')

h('3.5 Scenario Matrix and Procedure',2)
for t in ['1. Establish normal baselines under the defined grid states, including benign delay, packet loss, traffic load changes, and physical disturbances relevant to the simulator. Label each benign condition separately.','2. Run replay, FDI, and FCI scenarios in the isolated environment. Record attack start and stop, message type, endpoint, manipulation class, operating state, and ground-truth physical effect. Keep the scenario specification reproducible but confined to the laboratory.','3. Repeat each scenario across fixed seeds or repeated runs. Balance or document class prevalence and do not create a test set that is easier than deployment conditions.','4. Split data by run and, where feasible, by attack configuration, operating state, or topology. Preserve a held-out subset for generalisation testing.','5. Train and calibrate the network-only and cross-layer detectors using training and validation data only. Freeze detector settings before the test evaluation. Specify any adaptive update rule and bounds before validation.','6. Apply the rule-based detector with thresholds set on validation data or pre-registered engineering rules. Apply each detector to the same test cases.','7. Evaluate mitigation separately in paired detection-only and detection-plus-mitigation runs. Measure physical deviation, time to recovery, legitimate command disruption, and safety-limit violations.']:
    para(t)

h('3.6 Candidate Detection and Adaptation Logic',2)
para('The detector should first align observations and compute a feature vector x(t) from network telemetry, process measurements, operating context, and sensor-quality flags. A network-only baseline uses the network subset; a rule baseline applies documented limits; the proposed model uses both cyber and physical features. Candidate model families named in the proposal include logistic regression, random forest, gradient-boosted trees, and autoencoders. A pilot on training data should select a model using validation performance and interpretability requirements, not test-set outcomes.')
para('An interpretable output can be a calibrated attack probability p(t) accompanied by the principal contributing features and quality flags. An adaptive layer may update state-specific baselines or recalibrate thresholds only within a pre-approved envelope. Distribution shift should produce a drift warning and, when confidence is inadequate, a conservative operator-review state. Updates should be gated by trusted labels or reviewed data; uncertain alerts must not automatically become training labels. This limits feedback contamination and model poisoning risk. The framework’s adaptation is therefore bounded and auditable rather than unrestricted online learning.')

h('3.7 Mitigation Policy',2)
para('Mitigation is a separate experimental factor. Before testing, the testbed owner should define a state machine that maps alert confidence, corroborating physical evidence, control criticality, and sensor quality to permitted responses. A low-confidence alert may notify an operator and preserve evidence. A corroborated suspicious command may be held for validation or rejected only where the simulated control architecture has a safe alternative. Network isolation or fallback operation may be evaluated only if the testbed has validated that response. No action should bypass protection relays or independently change live grid settings.')
para('The primary mitigation outcome is the difference in a predefined physical-impact measure between matched attack runs with detection-only and with mitigation enabled. Secondary outcomes include time to containment, time to recovery, false intervention rate, and legitimate control disruption. Operational limits should be specified by the model and its owner; they must not be fabricated from generic thresholds.')

h('3.8 Data Analysis Plan',2)
para('For each detector, define a true positive (TP) as a correctly identified attack, a false positive (FP) as a benign case incorrectly flagged, a false negative (FN) as a missed attack, and a true negative (TN) as a correctly identified benign case. Calculate precision = TP/(TP+FP), recall = TP/(TP+FN), F1 = 2(precision × recall)/(precision + recall), and false-positive rate = FP/(FP+TN). Report confusion matrices and attack-class-specific metrics. Report accuracy only as supplementary information if class prevalence is imbalanced. Summarise detection delay and resource use using medians and upper percentiles when distributions are skewed. Report precision-recall curves or threshold trade-offs where useful, and confidence intervals from repeated trials or an appropriate resampling method.')
para('Use paired comparisons when detector outputs come from the same runs. Select inferential tests only after checking data structure and assumptions; report effect sizes and uncertainty rather than significance alone. For generalisation, report performance separately for seen and held-out scenarios and quantify the change. For mitigation, compare paired physical-impact outcomes and legitimate-operation costs. Failures, unsafe or unavailable responses, and negative results must be reported. These analyses cannot be completed until primary data exist.')

caption('Figure 2\nExperimental Workflow and Evidence Path')
flow=doc.add_table(rows=2,cols=4); flow.alignment=WD_TABLE_ALIGNMENT.CENTER; flow.style='Table Grid'
top=['Authorised isolated testbed','Scenario generation','Time-aligned labelled logs','Grouped train/validation/test']
bottom=['Three detector comparisons','Held-out condition evaluation','Safety-gated mitigation trials','Metrics, uncertainty, limitations']
for j in range(4):
    set_cell(flow.cell(0,j),top[j],True,(255,255,255),9); shade(flow.cell(0,j),'18364E')
    set_cell(flow.cell(1,j),bottom[j],False,None,8); shade(flow.cell(1,j),'EAF1F6')
note('Note. Author’s study protocol based on the supplied proposal. The current document completed the documentary and design stages; testbed and evaluation stages remain to be conducted.')

h('3.9 Validity, Ethics and Safety',2)
para('Internal validity depends on consistent labels, fixed comparison conditions, and leakage-resistant data splits. Construct validity depends on whether the simulated attacks, physical-impact measures, and benign conditions represent the stated concepts. External validity is limited by simulator fidelity, protocol implementation, asset diversity, and Nigeria-specific operational differences. Repeated scenarios, sensitivity analyses, transparent feature definitions, and explicit testbed assumptions can strengthen interpretation but cannot remove these limits.')
para('The work must remain within an authorised, isolated research environment. No utility, public network, or live operational asset should be probed or manipulated. Use synthetic or explicitly authorised data, follow institutional ethics and data-handling rules, restrict access to experiment artifacts, and document an emergency stop and reset procedure. Any future collaboration with a Nigerian utility requires written authorisation, safety review, and agreement on data ownership and disclosure. These protections are methodological conditions, not optional deployment advice.')

h('4 Results and Discussion',1)
h('4.1 Results of the Baseline Analysis',2)
para('The baseline analysis confirms four elements directly relevant to this work. First, Wlazlo et al. (2021) use an emulation-based cyber-physical testbed with a large synthetic grid model and DNP3 communication. Second, their investigated MiTM outcomes include false-data, false-command, and replay scenarios. Third, their detection design combines Snort IDS alerts with network monitoring, including delay and retransmission-related signals. Fourth, the paper explains that ordinary variation in industrial communication timing makes added latency an ambiguous indicator. These findings justify retaining the baseline’s attack taxonomy and testing network indicators alongside process evidence.')
para('The baseline also supports controlled experimentation: its testbed joins network emulation, DNP3 masters, and a real-time power-system simulator, with data from cyber and physical components. This creates an appropriate model for research reproducibility. The extension specified here is not to reproduce its attack scripts, but to compare detector classes, isolate unseen operating conditions, and measure mitigation outcomes under predefined safety constraints.')

h('4.2 Results of Nigerian Context Synthesis',2)
para('The Nigerian evidence establishes an active SCADA integration context. The Nigerian Electricity Regulatory Commission (NERC) reported in December 2024 that TCN was deploying an SCADA and Electricity Management System integrating transmission and distribution, while stakeholders discussed integration of power plants (NERC, 2024). The study therefore frames a segmented, evolving SCADA environment as the relevant deployment context and recommends staged, protocol-specific evaluation rather than assuming one model applies to every licensee or asset.')
para('NERC’s annual data for 2024 show that normal system behaviour is not equivalent to a narrow, static operating point: reported frequency bounds remained outside the normal band, and the report recorded nine system collapse incidents (NERC, 2025). This finding strengthens the need for benign disturbances and grid-state labels in a Nigerian detector evaluation. It does not show that cybersecurity caused the conditions. The research implication is to include the operating state as context and to test whether cross-layer evidence can avoid misclassifying a real physical disturbance as a cyberattack.')
para('The national cybersecurity strategy and Nigeria-focused assessment support a whole-of-system incident-response posture. ngCERT (2021) emphasizes critical information infrastructure resilience, while Ogundari et al. (2021) identify people, processes, and technology as necessary elements of an electricity-sector cybersecurity approach. In this proposed framework, those elements map to operator review and training, pre-approved response procedures, and monitored SCADA/OT technology. This is a design mapping, not a field finding about the readiness of a particular utility.')

h('4.3 Research Design Output',2)
para('The completed design output is a five-stage framework shown in Figure 1: collect network evidence; collect physical evidence; align and qualify the data; compute an adaptive cross-layer alert with confidence; and use a safety-gated response followed by recovery and review. It specifies the network-only and rule-based methods as comparison baselines, preserving a fair test of whether cross-layer evidence adds value. It also specifies separate detection and mitigation outcomes so that a high F1-score cannot be mistaken for safe operation.')
para('Table 1 operationalises the proposal’s independent, dependent, control, and confounding variables. Figure 2 lays out the experiment from isolation and scenario generation through grouped evaluation and reporting. Together these provide a reproducible procedure, but they are design artifacts; they do not constitute a completed testbed evaluation or evidence that the adaptive detector is effective.')

h('4.4 Hypothesis Status and Interpretation',2)
for t in ['1. H1 is untested. No paired detector outputs are available to compare the adaptive cross-layer F1-score with the network-only baseline.','2. H2 is untested. A lower false-positive rate at comparable recall requires validation data and a declared operating threshold for each detector.','3. H3 is untested. It requires separate seen and held-out condition test sets and a defined measure of performance decline.','4. H4 is untested. It requires matched detection-only and mitigation-enabled runs with physical-impact and legitimate-disruption measures.']:
    para(t)
para('The evidence nevertheless supports the logic of testing these hypotheses. Wlazlo et al. (2021) describe timing ambiguity and use multiple alerts; the Nigerian operating context provides a further reason to include benign network and physical variation; and OT guidance requires safety and availability to remain part of the evaluation (Stouffer et al., 2023). That combined rationale justifies the experiment but does not establish its outcome.')

h('4.5 Discussion of the Proposed Framework',2)
para('The main technical design choice is cross-layer correlation with explicit data-quality handling. Physical evidence can reduce ambiguity when a message-level anomaly coincides with a process response inconsistent with the expected command or state. Conversely, a physical deviation without supporting cyber evidence may be a real disturbance, a sensor fault, or a model mismatch. The detector should therefore report confidence and evidence provenance, and the operator response should account for sensor quality and operating state.')
para('Adaptation is useful only if the system remains auditable. A static global threshold may be brittle as traffic and operating conditions change, but unrestricted online learning can absorb attacks or incorrect labels as normal behaviour. The proposed compromise is bounded state-aware recalibration, drift alerts, and reviewed updates. Whether that compromise improves generalisation remains an empirical question.')
para('For Nigeria, feasibility also depends on data and governance. The SCADA transition creates an opportunity to define consistent timestamps, event labels, and monitoring interfaces, but a study cannot assume comprehensive real-time access or uniform assets. A pilot should begin with one authorised test segment, record vendor and protocol boundaries, and involve operations staff in defining safe response limits. This supports staged research without overstating whole-grid applicability.')

h('4.6 Limitations of the Present Results',2)
para('The result set is limited to evidence synthesis and research-design output. No labelled dataset, emulated traffic, trained detector, statistical comparison, or mitigation experiment was available in the supplied materials or generated during this assignment. Therefore, no numerical performance values, confidence intervals, significance tests, or operational benefits are asserted. The hypotheses remain untested. The documentary synthesis may also omit relevant studies not captured in the selected sources, and Nigeria-level policy or regulator evidence cannot substitute for utility-specific system documentation. These limitations should be resolved by implementing the controlled experiment and expanding the literature review before making empirical or deployment claims.')

h('5 Conclusion and Recommendations',1)
h('5.1 Conclusion',2)
para('This research work has developed a Nigeria-focused, proposal-aligned framework and evaluation methodology for adaptive detection and mitigation of stealthy MiTM attacks in power systems. It uses Wlazlo et al. (2021) as the baseline and retains the study’s focus on DNP3, replay, FDI, FCI, network monitoring, and cyber-physical testbed evaluation. It extends that foundation by specifying a comparative adaptive cross-layer detector, held-out-condition testing, Nigerian benign operating variation, and separate safety-aware mitigation outcomes.')
para('The work completed here is a documentary analysis and experimental design, not a primary performance study. The evidence supports the need for a fair test but does not show that cross-layer adaptation improves F1-score, false alarms, generalisation, or physical impact. The four hypotheses remain open. This distinction protects the credibility of the research and identifies the next step needed for a thesis with empirical results.')

h('5.2 Recommendations',2)
for t in ['1. Implement the stated experiment in an isolated and authorised cyber-physical testbed before reporting hypothesis outcomes. Publish the simulator, protocol, topology, sampling, labels, scenario matrix, and run configuration.','2. Keep the rule-based, network-only, and adaptive cross-layer methods on identical grouped train, validation, and test partitions. Reserve attack configurations or operating conditions for held-out testing and report per-class precision, recall, F1, false-positive rate, latency, and resource use.','3. Include Nigerian-relevant benign variation such as communication delay and loss, traffic changes, and physical disturbances, and label these conditions independently from attacks. Use current operational limits supplied by the testbed owner rather than inferring limits from national aggregate statistics.','4. Define the mitigation action set, safety constraints, and emergency stop with testbed engineers before testing. Measure physical deviation reduction and legitimate-operation disruption in paired runs. Keep automated actions in simulation unless separately authorised and safety validated.','5. Engage Nigerian power-sector operators, Nigerian Independent System Operator (NISO)/TCN, generation and distribution stakeholders, Nigerian research labs, and relevant cybersecurity bodies to establish an authorised pilot, data governance, incident reporting, and utility-specific interpretation.','6. Treat data quality and model governance as first-class controls: verify time alignment, protect labels, record drift, require review for updates, preserve audit trails, and report failures and negative results.','7. Stage future research by protocol and asset class. Begin with a reproducible DNP3 benchmark aligned to the baseline, then separately validate transfer to IEC 61850, Modbus, or other locally deployed systems.']:
    para(t)

h('5.3 Achievement of the Aim and Specific Objectives',2)
para('The aim and objectives have been achieved at the design, documentary-analysis, and evidence-synthesis level of this research work. The adaptive cross-layer framework, Nigerian context, experimental comparison, variable definitions, safety gates, and analysis plan are now specified. The performance-evaluation component is not empirically complete because no paired detector outputs or authorised testbed data were available. Accordingly, achievements below distinguish completed research-design outcomes from measurements that require subsequent execution.')
for t in ['1. Objective 1 is achieved. The baseline analysis documents Wlazlo et al.’s DNP3 testbed, replay, false-data injection and false-command injection scenarios, Snort and network-monitoring signals, and the ambiguity of timing-based alerts.','2. Objective 2 is achieved. Candidate network features and physical measurements are identified and operationalised, including timing, retransmissions, protocol events, voltage, frequency, power flow, breaker state, and state-estimation residuals, subject to testbed availability.','3. Objective 3 is achieved at the framework-design level. Figure 1 specifies aligned network and physical evidence, confidence-aware adaptive detection, drift handling, and safety-gated response. Its detection performance remains to be tested.','4. Objective 4 is achieved as a comparative experimental design. The rule-based, network-only, and adaptive cross-layer detectors are assigned a shared scenario matrix and leakage-resistant evaluation plan. Comparative empirical results remain pending.','5. Objective 5 is achieved as an evaluation protocol. Detection metrics, generalisation tests, compute measures, mitigation outcomes, and operational constraints are defined. Numerical evaluation and statistical conclusions require the planned experiment.']:
    para(t)

h('5.4 Answers to the Research Questions',2)
for t in ['1. Research Question 1: Wlazlo et al. (2021) used an emulation-based cyber-physical testbed, a synthetic grid, DNP3, and replay, false-data injection, and false-command injection scenarios. Their detection approach combined Snort intrusion-detection system (IDS) alerts with network monitoring, including delay and retransmission behaviour. A key limitation motivating this study is that added latency can resemble ordinary industrial communication variation; the present work also identifies the need to test generalisation and mitigation outcomes separately.','2. Research Question 2: The evidence supports cross-layer fusion as a plausible way to add process context to network-only evidence, but it does not establish that fusion improves F1-score in this study. The answer is therefore theoretically promising but empirically undetermined until paired predictions are evaluated on the same held-out cases.','3. Research Question 3: The research specifies a grouped held-out evaluation by attack configuration, operating condition, or topology, but no test outputs are available. Generalisation performance and the size of any decline cannot yet be reported.','4. Research Question 4: Correlating network alerts with physical-state consistency may help distinguish malicious manipulation from benign delay or disturbance. Whether this lowers false alarms at preserved recall is untested and requires paired predictions with benign delays, packet loss, and physical disturbances represented in the test set.','5. Research Question 5: A predefined response policy with validated actions can be tested for reduced attack impact while tracking recovery time and legitimate-control disruption. This research specifies that comparison but cannot claim measured reduction or safety until mitigation-enabled runs are completed.']:
    para(t)

h('5.5 Final Synthesis',2)
para('The stated aim is to design and evaluate an adaptive cyber-physical detection and mitigation framework for stealthy MiTM attacks in power-system communication and control environments, using Wlazlo et al. (2021) as the baseline and adapting the experimental context to Nigeria. The design and Nigeria-focused adaptation are completed in this work: the baseline’s DNP3 attack cases and monitoring signals are retained; Nigerian SCADA integration and normal operating variation shape the scenario design; and the framework joins network evidence, physical evidence, adaptive confidence scoring, and safety-gated response. The evaluation methodology is fully specified through shared detector baselines, grouped held-out testing, operational variables, detection metrics, and paired mitigation outcomes. The empirical evaluation part of the aim remains pending access to paired detector predictions or an authorised testbed. The work therefore achieves the research-design aim and all five objectives at the stated design-and-evidence level, while reporting the uncompleted measurement stage transparently.')

